## SGD(Stochastic Gradient Descent) Algoritham
Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) is an optimization algorithm used to minimize the loss function in machine learning models by updating model parameters (weights and biases) using one training example at a time.
🔹 Purpose of SGD

SGD is used to:

Train machine learning models (especially neural networks)
Find optimal values of weights
Reduce prediction error (loss)
🔹 Working Principle

SGD works by iteratively updating weights in the direction that reduces the loss.

Basic Steps:
Initialize weights randomly
Pick one training example (x, y)
Compute prediction
Calculate loss (error)
Compute gradient of loss
Update weights
Repeat for all data points


import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import tensorflow as tf
from tensorflow.keras.models import Sequential
from tensorflow.keras.layers import Dense

# 1. Create dataset (2 classes like your image)
np.random.seed(0)

class1 = np.random.randn(200, 2) + np.array([-10, -4])
class2 = np.random.randn(200, 2) + np.array([-2, 5])

X = np.vstack((class1, class2))
y = np.array([0]*200 + [1]*200)

# 2. Plot Data
plt.figure(figsize=(6,5))
plt.scatter(X[:,0], X[:,1], c=y)
plt.title("Data")
plt.show()

# 3. Build Model (Feedforward NN)
model = Sequential([
    Dense(8, input_dim=2, activation='relu'),
    Dense(1, activation='sigmoid')
])

# 4. Compile with SGD
model.compile(optimizer=tf.keras.optimizers.SGD(learning_rate=0.01),
              loss='binary_crossentropy')

# 5. Train model
history = model.fit(X, y, epochs=100, verbose=0)

# 6. Plot Training Loss
plt.figure(figsize=(6,5))
plt.plot(history.history['loss'])
plt.title("Training Loss")
plt.xlabel("Epoch")
plt.ylabel("Loss")
plt.show()
